## 17. AWS AI/ML Mapping

### Amazon Bedrock (Proposed LLM Alternative)

ClusterMind currently uses Groq for LLM inference. On AWS, the equivalent service is **Amazon Bedrock**:

- **Appropriate models:** Meta Llama 3.3 70B Instruct (available on Bedrock), Anthropic Claude 3 Sonnet (stronger reasoning), Amazon Titan (AWS-native)
- **Why Bedrock:** AWS-native IAM authentication (no API key management), VPC endpoints (private inference), model selection marketplace, Guardrails for responsible AI, Knowledge Bases for RAG
- **Why Groq instead:** Groq's LPU delivers 3–5x lower latency than Bedrock for the same model. For a real-time troubleshooting tool, this speed advantage is significant.

**"Why Bedrock instead of SageMaker?"** — Bedrock is managed inference — you pick a model and call an API. SageMaker is for custom model training, fine-tuning, and hosting your own model on dedicated endpoints. ClusterMind doesn't train models; it uses pre-trained Llama for inference. Bedrock is the right choice.

**"When would SageMaker be needed?"** — If we wanted to fine-tune Llama on a custom dataset of K8s incident reports to improve diagnosis quality, we'd use SageMaker for the training/fine-tuning job, then either deploy the fine-tuned model on a SageMaker endpoint or import it into Bedrock.

